+# Design analysis
+# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
+#    The original task locates a hidden 3x3 cell-level blueprint and completes every blueprint-active
+#    position with the minority cell pattern. D5 changes the write result so active positions become
+#    background-colored holes; D16 changes it so active positions toggle between majority and minority.
+#    The fused rule keeps the same discovery pipeline but changes the toggle endpoints: within the
+#    placed blueprint, a currently visible minority clue cell is toggled off by being erased to the
+#    divider color, while a currently ordinary cell is toggled on by being painted with the minority
+#    pattern. Example: if the hidden mask has 6 active positions and the input shows 3 minority clues,
+#    the original output would show 6 minority cells, D5 would show 6 holes, D16 would show 3 majority
+#    restorations plus 3 new minority cells, and this combo shows 3 holes plus 3 new minority cells.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    The 7x7 lattice, 3x3 cell interiors, 3x3 hidden mask, 6-7 active pixels, and 3-4 visible clues
+#    are preserved. That keeps placement inference as hard as the base task while making the visual
+#    effect clearly different from both completion and pure erasure: every sample contains both newly
+#    painted minority cells and freshly carved holes inside one coherent placed block.
+# c) Implicit assumption audit:
+#    - `_infer_cell_grid_params` only relies on full divider rows/cols, and carving happens strictly
+#      inside cell interiors, so divider inference still works.
+#    - Counting cell patterns still assumes the input has only majority and minority clue cells; the
+#      generator keeps holes out of the input so this remains true.
+#    - `active_color = argmax(...)` still assumes the minority-exclusive color dominates the 3x3 clue;
+#      the generator keeps active counts at 6 or 7 to preserve that.
+#    - The original final overwrite loop assumed active -> minority. That assumption breaks here: the
+#      solver must inspect whether the current active-grid cell is already a minority clue and then
+#      choose erased-hole or minority-paint accordingly.
+#    - Inactive positions inside the placed 3x3 block are forced back to majority to avoid accidental
+#      dependence on input leftovers; the generator always uses a majority base so this is consistent.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    The hidden 3x3 blueprint is still placed at offsets 0..4 inside the 7x7 cell grid, so it never
+#    crosses boundaries. Input clue cells are chosen only from active blueprint positions and must
+#    uniquely determine the placement. Output rewrites only whole 3x3 interiors at legal cell origins,
+#    using either the minority texture or a solid divider-colored hole, so no overlap ambiguity occurs.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    A human still reads the minority clue cell as the miniature blueprint. Comparing input/output now
+#    reveals a structured two-way change inside the same enlarged region: some clue cells disappear
+#    into holes while complementary positions light up as minority cells. This paired disappearance/
